{% extends "shell.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}{{ copy.switcher_title }} · {{ copy.product }}{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
{#
  `RCA-002` FR-051. This renders exactly what the reader returned and never filters here: a filter
  in the template would be a second place the membership rule is decided, and the two could
  disagree. The reader's query is the single definition of "organizations this actor belongs to".

  No count is rendered. "You belong to 3 organizations" says something about the actor's
  memberships that the list already says, and a count that disagreed with the list would be a
  disclosure about rows the list withheld.
#}
<section class="document-card" aria-labelledby="page-title">
  <h1 id="page-title">{{ copy.switcher_title }}</h1>
  <p class="lede">{{ copy.switcher_intro }}</p>
  <ul class="organization-list">
    {% for organization in organizations %}
    <li class="organization-item">
      <a id="organization-name-{{ loop.index }}"
         href="{{ prefix }}/{{ language }}/{{ organization.organization_id }}/team">
        {{ organization.name }}
      </a>
      {#
        The entry point into the journey, once per organization rather than once per page. A single
        action had to name a target, and it named `organizations[0]` -- so a member of several
        organizations opened an analysis in whichever one the query returned first, with nothing on
        screen saying which. The choice is the reader's here because the action sits on the row
        whose organization it acts on.

        This discloses nothing the list above does not: the actions are exactly as many as the
        links, so the reticence about counts holds unchanged.

        A POST rather than a link because it opens an analysis, and a GET that mutates is a GET a
        browser may prefetch.
      #}
      <form method="post"
            action="{{ prefix }}/{{ language }}/{{ organization.organization_id }}/analyses">
        {#
          Named by both spans in source order, so the accessible name is "Start a new analysis
          Acme" and not the bare label repeated once per row. `aria-labelledby` rather than an
          `aria-label` string because it composes the two from copy the translators already own --
          an `aria-label` would need a separator and a word order invented here, in a template
          that also renders Arabic.
        #}
        <button type="submit"
                aria-labelledby="new-analysis-{{ loop.index }} organization-name-{{ loop.index }}">
          <span id="new-analysis-{{ loop.index }}">{{ copy.new_analysis }}</span>
        </button>
      </form>
    </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
</section>
{% endblock %}
